# Growing a list that stands in a field, in place. `xs @ xs.push(v)` grows
# a list in place only where `xs` is a plain name; through a field, even a
# field of an `@` parameter (`l.entries @ l.entries.push(e)`), it copies the
# whole list at every push (spec § 10). Measured on this Mac on 2026-11-26:
# 100,000 pushes through a field of an `@` record took 32 s of user time,
# and the same pushes into that field lent to an `@` parameter, as `grow`
# takes it, under 0.01 s. Rebel's `lists` answers new lists and has no push
# in place, so this one function stands here, the program's own.

## Growing

# Adds `value` at the end of `xs`, in place.
#
# - `xs`: the list, which the caller lends, a field of its own included.
# - `value`: the element added.
function grow<T>(@xs: [T], value: T)
    xs @ xs.push(value)

## Tests

test "grow: a field lent and grown in place"
    held @= Held(items: [1])
    grow(@held.items, value: 2)
    assert held.items == [1, 2]

# A record holding a list, for the test.
record Held
    # The list.
    items: [i64]
